package com.rork.rockscout.data

import com.rork.rockscout.ui.components.DinoBodyPlan

/** Part 2 of the dinosaur dictionary entries (pterosaurs, marine reptiles, ice age mammals, and other prehistoric animals). */
internal object DinoEntriesPart2 {

    val entries: List<DinoEntry> = listOf(

        // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        // PTEROSAURS — Flying reptiles (NOT dinosaurs)
        // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

        DinoEntry("pteranodon","Pteranodon",DinoEra.CRETACEOUS,"Late Cretaceous","~86–84 mya",DinoDiet.PISCIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.PTEROSAUR,"23 ft wingspan (7 m)","44 lb","Seas of North America","The largest toothless pterosaur, with a 23-foot wingspan. Over 1,200 Pteranodon specimens have been found. Males had large backward-pointing crests; females had small ones. It soared over the Western Interior Seaway scooping fish.",listOf("Discovered 1876 by Othniel Charles Marsh","Over 1,200 specimens found","Males had large crests; females had small ones","NOT a dinosaur — a flying reptile","Could fly across the inland sea in a day"),listOf("Niobrara Chalk, Kansas, USA"),0xFF6FBF8A),
        DinoEntry("quetzalcoatlus","Quetzalcoatlus",DinoEra.CRETACEOUS,"Late Cretaceous","~68–66 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.PTEROSAUR,"36 ft wingspan (11 m)","440–550 lb","Plains of North America","The largest flying animal ever. Quetzalcoatlus stood as tall as a giraffe on the ground and had a 36-foot wingspan. Named after the Aztec feathered serpent god, it may have hunted small dinosaurs on foot like a giant stork.",listOf("Discovered 1975","Largest flying animal ever — 36-ft wingspan","Stood as tall as a giraffe on the ground","Named after the Aztec god Quetzalcoatl","May have hunted on the ground like a stork"),listOf("Javelina Formation, Big Bend, Texas, USA"),0xFF5FA070),
        DinoEntry("dimorphodon","Dimorphodon",DinoEra.JURASSIC,"Early Jurassic","~195–190 mya",DinoDiet.INSECTIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.PTEROSAUR,"4.5 ft wingspan (1.4 m)","3.3 lb","Coastal forests of England","An early pterosaur with a large puffin-like head and two types of teeth. Discovered by Mary Anning on the Jurassic Coast. Dimorphodon had a short wingspan and may have been a clumsy flyer that climbed trees.",listOf("Discovered 1828 by Mary Anning","Discovered by pioneering fossil hunter Mary Anning","Had a puffin-like head with two tooth types","May have been a tree-climbing pterosaur"),listOf("Lyme Regis, Dorset, England"),0xFF5F9060),
        DinoEntry("rhamphorhynchus","Rhamphorhynchus",DinoEra.JURASSIC,"Late Jurassic","~150 mya",DinoDiet.PISCIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.PTEROSAUR,"5.9 ft wingspan (1.8 m)","2.2 lb","Lagoon islands of Europe","A long-tailed pterosaur with needle-like teeth for catching fish. Rhamphorhynchus specimens from Solnhofen preserve wing membranes, showing it had a stiff tail with a diamond-shaped vane at the tip.",listOf("Discovered 1846","Wing membranes preserved in exquisite detail","Long stiff tail with a diamond vane","Caught fish in Jurassic lagoons"),listOf("Solnhofen Limestone, Bavaria, Germany"),0xFF6A9070),
        DinoEntry("tapejara","Tapejara",DinoEra.CRETACEOUS,"Early Cretaceous","~112 mya",DinoDiet.OMNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.PTEROSAUR,"16 ft wingspan (5 m)","50 lb","Coastal regions of Brazil","A colorful pterosaur with an enormous sail-like crest on its head. Tapejara may have been brightly colored, and its crest may have been used for display. Some specimens show crests as large as the head itself.",listOf("Discovered 1989","Enormous sail-like crest on its head","May have been brightly colored","Crest may have been for species recognition"),listOf("Araripe Basin, Brazil"),0xFF8A5A7A),
        DinoEntry("hatzegopteryx","Hatzegopteryx",DinoEra.CRETACEOUS,"Late Cretaceous","~70–66 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.PTEROSAUR,"33–36 ft wingspan (10–11 m)","550 lb","Islands of Europe","A giant pterosaur as large as Quetzalcoatlus but with a shorter, more robust neck. Hatzegopteryx lived on the island of Hateg in Europe, where it may have been the apex predator — hunting dwarf dinosaurs on foot.",listOf("Discovered 2002","As large as Quetzalcoatlus but more robust","Apex predator of a European island — hunted dwarf dinosaurs","May have stalked prey on the ground"),listOf("Ha\u021beg Basin, Romania"),0xFF6A8A7A),

        // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        // MARINE REPTILES (NOT dinosaurs)
        // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

        DinoEntry("elasmosaurus","Elasmosaurus",DinoEra.CRETACEOUS,"Late Cretaceous","~80 mya",DinoDiet.PISCIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ELASMOSAUR,"45 ft (14 m)","4 tons","Inland seas of North America","A plesiosaur with an astonishing 72 neck vertebrae — more than any other animal. Half its length was neck. When first described, Edward Cope put its head on the wrong end, starting a famous paleontological rivalry.",listOf("Discovered 1868 by Edward Drinker Cope","72 neck vertebrae — more than any animal","First reconstruction put the head on the tail — a famous mistake","Half its length was just neck"),listOf("Pierre Shale, Kansas, USA"),0xFF4078A0),
        DinoEntry("plesiosaurus","Plesiosaurus",DinoEra.JURASSIC,"Early Jurassic","~200–190 mya",DinoDiet.PISCIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.PLESIOSAUR,"15 ft (4.5 m)","1,100 lb","Seas of Europe","The first complete plesiosaur, discovered by Mary Anning in 1823. Plesiosaurus had a small head on a long neck, four paddle-like flippers, and 'flew' through the water. It inspired legends of the Loch Ness Monster.",listOf("Discovered 1823 by Mary Anning","Inspired the Loch Ness Monster legend","Swam using all four flippers in a 'flying' motion","Mary Anning discovered the first complete specimen"),listOf("Lyme Regis, Dorset, England"),0xFF4080A0),
        DinoEntry("ichthyosaurus","Ichthyosaurus",DinoEra.JURASSIC,"Early Jurassic","~200–190 mya",DinoDiet.PISCIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ICHTHYOSAUR,"11 ft (3.3 m)","2,200 lb","Seas of Europe","A dolphin-shaped marine reptile with enormous eyes for deep-sea hunting. Ichthyosaurus gave birth to live young — fossilized mothers with embryos have been found. Its eyes were the size of dinner plates.",listOf("Discovered 1821 by Mary Anning","Gave birth to live young — fossil embryos found","Eyes the size of dinner plates","Mary Anning discovered the first complete skeleton"),listOf("Lyme Regis, England","Holzmaden, Germany"),0xFF5090B0),
        DinoEntry("liopleurodon","Liopleurodon",DinoEra.JURASSIC,"Middle Jurassic","~166–160 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.PLESIOSAUR,"20–33 ft (6–10 m)","3–8 tons","Seas of Europe","A short-necked pliosaur with massive jaws and 4-inch teeth. Liopleurodon was an apex predator of Jurassic seas, hunting fish, squid, and other marine reptiles. Its size was exaggerated in 'Walking with Dinosaurs.'",listOf("Discovered 1873","Apex predator of the Jurassic seas","Teeth up to 4 inches long","Short neck, massive jaws — a pliosaur"),listOf("Oxford Clay, England","France"),0xFF306090),
        DinoEntry("mosasaurus","Mosasaurus",DinoEra.CRETACEOUS,"Late Cretaceous","~82–66 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.MOSASAUR,"56 ft (17 m)","15 tons","Seas worldwide","A giant marine lizard — not a dinosaur but a close relative of monitor lizards and snakes. Mosasaurus was the apex predator of the Late Cretaceous seas, hunting everything from fish to other mosasaurs. It went extinct with the dinosaurs.",listOf("Discovered 1764","Apex predator of the Cretaceous seas","Related to monitor lizards and snakes","Could unhinge its jaw to swallow large prey"),listOf("Maastricht, Netherlands","Kansas, USA","Morocco"),0xFF205080),
        DinoEntry("tylosaurus","Tylosaurus",DinoEra.CRETACEOUS,"Late Cretaceous","~85–80 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.MOSASAUR,"33–50 ft (10–15 m)","10 tons","Inland seas of North America","A large mosasaur that dominated the Western Interior Seaway of North America. Tylosaurus had a long, cone-shaped snout used for ramming prey. Stomach contents show it ate fish, sharks, birds, and other mosasaurs.",listOf("Discovered 1869 by Othniel Charles Marsh","Dominated the inland sea of North America","Used its snout to ram prey","Ate sharks and even other mosasaurs"),listOf("Kansas, USA","Alabama, USA"),0xFF306880),
        DinoEntry("plotosaurus","Plotosaurus",DinoEra.CRETACEOUS,"Late Cretaceous","~70 mya",DinoDiet.PISCIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.MOSASAUR,"30 ft (9 m)","3 tons","Seas of North America","A highly derived mosasaur with a streamlined, fish-like body and a tail fluke like a shark. Plotosaurus was one of the fastest and most specialized mosasaurs — the most evolutionarily advanced of its kind.",listOf("Discovered 1942 by Charles Lewis Camp","Most specialized mosasaur — shark-like tail fluke","Highly streamlined for speed","Most evolutionarily advanced mosasaur"),listOf("California, USA"),0xFF408090),
        DinoEntry("shonisaurus","Shonisaurus",DinoEra.TRIASSIC,"Late Triassic","~237–227 mya",DinoDiet.PISCIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ICHTHYOSAUR,"69 ft (21 m)","40 tons","Seas of North America","The largest ichthyosaur known — reaching 69 feet, as long as a sperm whale. Shonisaurus had a massive body and was a deep-sea predator. 37 individuals found together at Berlin-Ichthyosaur State Park in Nevada may represent a mass stranding.",listOf("Discovered 1928","Largest ichthyosaur — 69 ft, as long as a sperm whale","37 found together in Nevada — possible mass stranding","Nevada's state fossil"),listOf("Berlin-Ichthyosaur State Park, Nevada, USA"),0xFF5070A0),
        DinoEntry("shastasaurus","Shastasaurus",DinoEra.TRIASSIC,"Late Triassic","~235 mya",DinoDiet.PISCIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ICHTHYOSAUR,"69 ft (21 m)","40 tons","Seas of North America & Asia","The most specialized ichthyosaur — Shastasaurus had a short, toothless snout and may have been a suction feeder like modern beaked whales. It was among the largest marine reptiles ever.",listOf("Discovered 1895","Short, toothless snout — suction feeder","Among the largest marine reptiles ever","Fed like a modern beaked whale"),listOf("California, USA","China"),0xFF4060A0),
        DinoEntry("ophthalmosaurus","Ophthalmosaurus",DinoEra.JURASSIC,"Late Jurassic","~165–160 mya",DinoDiet.PISCIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ICHTHYOSAUR,"20 ft (6 m)","2 tons","Seas worldwide","An ichthyosaur with the largest eyes of any vertebrate — up to 9 inches across. Ophthalmosaurus could see in the deepest, darkest ocean waters. It was a streamlined deep-diver that gave birth to live young.",listOf("Discovered 1874","Largest eyes of any vertebrate — 9 inches across","Deep-diver of the Jurassic seas","Gave birth to live young in the water"),listOf("England","Argentina","USA"),0xFF5080B0),
        DinoEntry("kronosaurus","Kronosaurus",DinoEra.CRETACEOUS,"Early Cretaceous","~112 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.PLESIOSAUR,"30–33 ft (9–10 m)","10–12 tons","Seas of Australia","A massive pliosaur — a short-necked plesiosaur with a skull up to 9 feet long. Kronosaurus had large conical teeth for seizing large prey including other plesiosaurs. It was the apex predator of Australian Cretaceous seas.",listOf("Discovered 1899","Skull up to 9 feet long","Apex predator of Australian seas","Hunted other marine reptiles"),listOf("Queensland, Australia","Colombia"),0xFF406890),
        DinoEntry("sarcosuchus","Sarcosuchus",DinoEra.CRETACEOUS,"Early Cretaceous","~112 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.CROCODILIAN,"31–40 ft (9.5–12 m)","3–8 tons","Rivers of North Africa","A giant crocodile that rivaled dinosaurs in size. Sarcosuchus, also called 'SuperCroc,' had a 6-foot skull and may have preyed on dinosaurs that came to drink. It lived alongside Spinosaurus and Suchomimus.",listOf("Discovered 1964","'SuperCroc' — 40 ft long giant crocodile","May have preyed on dinosaurs at water's edge","6-foot skull with conical teeth"),listOf("Gadoufaoua, Niger","Brazil"),0xFF5A7A4A),
        DinoEntry("deinosuchus","Deinosuchus",DinoEra.CRETACEOUS,"Late Cretaceous","~82–73 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.CROCODILIAN,"33–40 ft (10–12 m)","5–8 tons","Rivers of North America","A giant alligator relative that was even larger than Sarcosuchus. Deinosuchus preyed on dinosaurs — bite marks on tyrannosaur bones show it even attacked young T. rex. It was the terror of Cretaceous rivers.",listOf("Discovered 1858","Giant crocodile that hunted dinosaurs","Bite marks found on tyrannosaur bones","Even young T. rex feared it"),listOf("Texas, USA","Montana, USA","North Carolina, USA"),0xFF4A6A3A),
        DinoEntry("megalodon","Megalodon",DinoEra.NEOGENE,"Miocene–Pliocene","~23–3.6 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.SHARK_GIANT,"50–60 ft (15–18 m)","50–70 tons","Seas worldwide","The largest shark and largest predatory fish ever. Megalodon had 7-inch teeth and preyed on whales for 20 million years. Its bite force was the strongest of any animal — stronger than T. rex. It went extinct when oceans cooled.",listOf("Discovered 1843","Largest shark ever — 60 ft, 3x a great white","Teeth up to 7 inches — the size of a human hand","Bite force stronger than T. rex","Hunted whales worldwide"),listOf("North Carolina, USA","Peru","Japan","Florida, USA"),0xFF6090B0),
        DinoEntry("leedsichthys","Leedsichthys",DinoEra.JURASSIC,"Middle Jurassic","~165 mya",DinoDiet.FILTER_FEEDER,DinoBodyPlan.SHARK_GIANT,"50 ft (16.5 m)","50 tons","Seas of Europe & South America","The largest bony fish ever — a 50-foot filter feeder like a giant Jurassic whale shark. Leedsichthys swam through the same seas as Liopleurodon, filtering plankton with enormous gill rakers. Its skeleton was so delicate that it is difficult to reconstruct.",listOf("Discovered 1886 by Alfred Nicholson Leeds","Largest bony fish ever — 50 ft filter feeder","Swam the same Jurassic seas as Liopleurodon","Filtered plankton like a whale shark"),listOf("Oxford Clay, Peterborough, England","France","Chile"),0xFF5090A0),

        // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        // PALEOGENE & NEOGENE (66–2.6 mya) — Age of Mammals
        // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

        DinoEntry("basilosaurus","Basilosaurus",DinoEra.PALEOGENE,"Late Eocene","~41–34 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.CROCODILIAN,"60 ft (18 m)","10 tons","Seas of North Africa & North America","An early whale with a serpentine body and tiny hind limbs — vestigial legs that link it to land ancestors. When first discovered it was mistaken for a marine reptile, hence the name 'king lizard.'",listOf("Discovered 1834","Had tiny functional hind limbs — evidence of whale evolution from land","Mistaken for a marine reptile — name means 'king lizard'","Alabama's state fossil","Wadi Al-Hitan in Egypt is filled with Basilosaurus fossils"),listOf("Alabama, USA","Wadi Al-Hitan, Egypt"),0xFF6090A0),
        DinoEntry("paraceratherium","Paraceratherium",DinoEra.PALEOGENE,"Oligocene","~34–23 mya",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.RHINO_GIANT,"26 ft (8 m)","15–20 tons","Plains of Asia","The largest land mammal ever — a hornless rhinoceros relative that stood 18 feet at the shoulder and weighed 20 tons. Paraceratherium browsed treetops like a mammalian sauropod across Oligocene Asia.",listOf("Discovered 1911 by Clive Forster Cooper","Largest land mammal ever — 18 ft at the shoulder","Weighed 20 tons — larger than any elephant","Had no horn — a giant hornless rhino","Browsed treetops like a mammalian sauropod"),listOf("Mongolia","Kazakhstan","Pakistan","China"),0xFF908070),
        DinoEntry("andrewsarchus","Andrewsarchus",DinoEra.PALEOGENE,"Eocene","~45 mya",DinoDiet.OMNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.WOLF_PREHISTORIC,"10 ft (3 m)","1,000 lb","Plains of Mongolia","Known only from a single 33-inch skull — the largest carnivorous mammal skull ever found. Andrewsarchus may have been the largest carnivorous land mammal, but without a skeleton, its true size and lifestyle remain a mystery.",listOf("Discovered 1923 by Roy Chapman Andrews","Known only from one enormous skull","May have been the largest carnivorous land mammal","Its lifestyle is still a mystery","Named after explorer Roy Chapman Andrews"),listOf("Gobi Desert, Mongolia"),0xFF5A4A3A),
        DinoEntry("entelodont","Entelodont",DinoEra.PALEOGENE,"Oligocene","~33–23 mya",DinoDiet.OMNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.WOLF_PREHISTORIC,"6.5 ft (2 m)","1,000 lb","Plains of North America & Asia","The 'hell pig' or 'terminator pig' — a pig-like omnivore the size of a bison with bone-crushing jaws and bony knobs on its skull. Despite the nickname, entelodonts were more closely related to hippos and whales than to pigs.",listOf("Discovered 1873","'Terminator pig' — size of a bison","More closely related to whales and hippos than pigs","Jaws could crush bone and turtle shells"),listOf("South Dakota, USA","Nebraska, USA","Mongolia"),0xFF706050),
        DinoEntry("arsinoitherium","Arsinoitherium",DinoEra.PALEOGENE,"Oligocene","~30 mya",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.RHINO_GIANT,"11.5 ft (3.5 m)","3 tons","Forests of North Africa","A large herbivore with two enormous side-by-side horns on its snout and smaller horns behind. Despite its rhinoceros-like appearance, Arsinoitherium was related to elephants and hyraxes. It lived in lush Oligocene forests of Egypt.",listOf("Discovered 1902","Two massive side-by-side horns on its snout","Related to elephants, not rhinos","Lived in ancient Egyptian forests","Named after Queen Arsinoe of Egypt"),listOf("Fayum, Egypt"),0xFF8A7A6A),
        DinoEntry("titanoboa","Titanoboa",DinoEra.PALEOGENE,"Paleocene","~60–58 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.CROCODILIAN,"42–50 ft (13–15 m)","2,500 lb","Tropical rivers of South America","The largest snake ever — longer than a school bus. Titanoboa lived just after the dinosaur extinction when tropical temperatures were higher, allowing cold-blooded animals to grow enormous. It hunted crocodiles and large fish.",listOf("Discovered 2009","Largest snake ever — 42 to 50 ft long","Hunted crocodiles and large fish","Lived just after the dinosaur extinction","Warmer climate allowed its enormous size"),listOf("Cerrej\u00f3n, Colombia"),0xFF4A6A4A),
        DinoEntry("phorusrhacos","Phorusrhacos",DinoEra.NEOGENE,"Miocene","~20–13 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.BIRD_PREHISTORIC,"8 ft (2.5 m)","300 lb","Plains of South America","The 'terror bird' — a flightless apex predator with a massive hooked beak that could crush bone. Phorusrhacos dominated South American grasslands for millions of years after the dinosaurs went extinct. It could run down prey at high speed.",listOf("Discovered 1887 by Florentino Ameghino","8-ft flightless apex predator with a bone-crushing beak","Dominated South America after the dinosaurs","Could run down prey at 30+ mph","Related to modern seriemas"),listOf("Santa Cruz Formation, Patagonia, Argentina"),0xFFB06050),
        DinoEntry("kelenken","Kelenken",DinoEra.NEOGENE,"Miocene","~15 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.BIRD_PREHISTORIC,"10 ft (3 m)","350 lb","Plains of South America","The largest terror bird — with a skull 28 inches long, the largest bird skull known. Kelenken was a towering flightless predator that dominated Miocene Patagonia. Its enormous beak could deliver devastating pecks.",listOf("Discovered 2006","Largest terror bird — 28-inch skull","Largest bird skull ever found","Towering 10-ft flightless predator","Name comes from a Tehuelche mythological beast"),listOf("Comallo, R\u00edo Negro, Argentina"),0xFFA05040),
        DinoEntry("argentavis","Argentavis",DinoEra.NEOGENE,"Late Miocene","~6 mya",DinoDiet.SCAVENGER,DinoBodyPlan.PTEROSAUR,"24 ft wingspan (7.3 m)","160 lb","Skies of South America","The largest flying bird ever — with a 24-foot wingspan comparable to a small plane. Argentavis soared on thermal updrafts over Miocene Argentina, gliding for hundreds of miles without flapping. It may have been a scavenger like a condor.",listOf("Discovered 1980","Largest flying bird ever — 24-ft wingspan","Soared on thermals — rarely needed to flap","Weighed about 160 lbs","Comparable to a Cessna airplane"),listOf("Argentina"),0xFFA06050),
        DinoEntry("deinotherium","Deinotherium",DinoEra.NEOGENE,"Miocene–Pleistocene","~20–1 mya",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.MAMMOTH,"13–16 ft (4–5 m)","8–13 tons","Savannas of Africa, Europe & Asia","A massive prehistoric elephant with downward-curving tusks on its lower jaw — unlike any modern elephant. Deinotherium used its strange tusks to dig up roots and strip bark. It survived until about 1 million years ago.",listOf("Discovered 1829","Tusks curved DOWN from the lower jaw — unique among elephants","Used tusks to dig roots and strip bark","Survived until ~1 million years ago","One of the largest proboscideans ever"),listOf("Kenya","Romania","Germany","India"),0xFF6B5040),
        DinoEntry("chalicotherium","Chalicotherium",DinoEra.NEOGENE,"Miocene","~20–13 mya",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.SLOTH_GIANT,"8.5 ft (2.6 m)","1,300 lb","Forests of Europe & Asia","A bizarre mammal with the body of a horse, the head of a tapir, and the claws of a sloth. Chalicotherium had large hooked claws instead of hooves and walked on its knuckles like a gorilla. It used its claws to pull down branches.",listOf("Discovered 1893","Horse body, tapir head, sloth claws — a chimera","Walked on its knuckles like a gorilla","Used claws to pull down branches","Claws instead of hooves — unique among perissodactyls"),listOf("Germany","France","China"),0xFF7A6A5A),
        DinoEntry("megatherium","Megatherium",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~5 million–10,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.SLOTH_GIANT,"20 ft (6 m)","4 tons","Forests of South America","The giant ground sloth — one of the largest land mammals ever. Megatherium stood 20 feet tall on its hind legs and could pull down entire trees. It had enormous claws and may have been partly bipedal. It survived until humans arrived in the Americas.",listOf("Discovered 1789","Stood 20 ft tall on hind legs — larger than an elephant","Could pull down entire trees with its claws","Survived until humans arrived in the Americas","One of the largest land mammals ever"),listOf("Argentina","Brazil","Uruguay","Peru"),0xFF8B7A5A),
        DinoEntry("megalania","Megalania",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~2.5 million–50,000 years",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.CROCODILIAN,"20 ft (6 m)","1,000–2,000 lb","Plains of Australia","The largest terrestrial lizard ever — a giant monitor lizard related to the Komodo dragon. Megalania was the apex predator of Pleistocene Australia, hunting giant kangaroos and diprotodons. It likely had a venomous bite like modern monitors.",listOf("Discovered 1859","Largest land lizard ever — 20 ft long","Apex predator of Pleistocene Australia","Likely venomous like its Komodo dragon relatives","Coexisted with early Aboriginal Australians"),listOf("Queensland, Australia","New South Wales, Australia"),0xFF5A4030),
        DinoEntry("pelagornis","Pelagornis",DinoEra.NEOGENE,"Miocene–Pliocene","~25–2.5 mya",DinoDiet.PISCIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.PTEROSAUR,"24 ft wingspan (7.4 m)","80 lb","Seas worldwide","A giant seabird with bony 'teeth' — one of the largest flying birds of all time. Pelagornis had a 24-foot wingspan and soared over Miocene oceans for millions of years, hunting fish and squid. It went extinct during the Pliocene.",listOf("Discovered 1857","One of the largest flying birds ever","Had bony 'teeth' — not true teeth","24-foot wingspan — soared over ancient oceans","Hunted fish and squid across the globe"),listOf("South Carolina, USA","England","Australia","Morocco"),0xFF8A8A7A),
        DinoEntry("terror-bird-titanis","Titanis",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pliocene–Pleistocene","~5 million–11,000 years",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.BIRD_PREHISTORIC,"8 ft (2.5 m)","330 lb","Plains of North America","A North American terror bird — one of the few top predators from South America that migrated north after the Panama land bridge formed. Titanis had a massive hooked beak and could run at high speed. It went extinct about 11,000 years ago.",listOf("Discovered 1963","Only terror bird known from North America","Migrated north after Panama land bridge formed","Massive hooked beak could crush bone","Went extinct ~11,000 years ago"),listOf("Florida, USA","Texas, USA"),0xFFA06050),
        DinoEntry("uintatherium","Uintatherium",DinoEra.PALEOGENE,"Eocene","~45–37 mya",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.RHINO_GIANT,"13 ft (4 m)","2 tons","Forests of North America","A bizarre early mammal with six bony knobs on its skull and large saber-like upper canines. Uintatherium was one of the first large mammals after the dinosaur extinction. It had a small brain despite its massive body.",listOf("Discovered 1872","Six bony knobs on its skull and saber-like canines","One of the first large mammals after dinosaurs","Had a remarkably small brain","Named after the Uinta Mountains of Utah"),listOf("Wyoming, USA","Utah, USA","China"),0xFF7A7A6A),
        DinoEntry("amphicyon","Amphicyon",DinoEra.NEOGENE,"Miocene","~15–9 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.BEAR_GIANT,"8 ft (2.5 m)","600 lb","Forests of Europe, Asia & North America","The 'bear dog' — neither a bear nor a dog, but a member of an extinct family that combined traits of both. Amphicyon was a powerful predator that hunted like a bear, using strength rather than speed. It was an apex predator of the Miocene.",listOf("Discovered 1836","'Bear dog' — neither a bear nor a dog","Powerful predator that hunted like a bear","Apex predator of the Miocene","Went extinct when true carnivores diversified"),listOf("France","Germany","Nebraska, USA"),0xFF7A6A5A),
        DinoEntry("merychippus","Merychippus",DinoEra.NEOGENE,"Miocene","~17–11 mya",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ORNITHOPOD,"5 ft (1.5 m)","220 lb","Plains of North America","A critical horse ancestor — the first horse to have a single hoof per foot while still retaining side toes. Merychippus had high-crowned teeth for grazing grass, which was spreading across North America during the Miocene.",listOf("Discovered 1857","First horse with a single hoof per foot","High-crowned teeth for grazing grass","Had side toes that no longer touched the ground","Critical link in horse evolution"),listOf("Nebraska, USA","Florida, USA","Texas, USA"),0xFF9A8A6A),
        DinoEntry("synthetoceras","Synthetoceras",DinoEra.NEOGENE,"Miocene","~10–5 mya",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ORNITHOPOD,"8 ft (2.4 m)","600 lb","Plains of North America","A bizarre protoceratid with a Y-shaped horn on its snout and a pair of horns behind its eyes. Synthetoceras was a distant relative of camels and deer, but with a truly strange head. Its forked nasal horn may have been used for display or fighting.",listOf("Discovered 1935","Y-shaped horn on its snout — truly bizarre","Distant relative of camels and deer","Forked nasal horn for display or fighting","No living relatives — a uniquely American lineage"),listOf("Texas, USA","Nebraska, USA","Florida, USA"),0xFFA0805A),

        // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        // QUATERNARY — THE ICE AGE
        // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

        DinoEntry("woolly-mammoth","Woolly Mammoth",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~400,000–4,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.MAMMOTH,"13 ft (4 m)","6 tons","Tundra of the Northern Hemisphere","The iconic Ice Age giant — covered in thick fur with a fat hump for energy storage and 15-foot curved tusks. Frozen carcasses in Siberian permafrost preserve hair, skin, and even stomach contents. The last mammoths survived on Wrangel Island until just 4,000 years ago.",listOf("Discovered 1799","Frozen carcasses with hair, skin, and stomach contents preserved","Last mammoths lived on Wrangel Island just 4,000 years ago","Tusks up to 15 feet long","Mammoth ivory is still found in Siberia today"),listOf("Siberia, Russia","Alaska, USA","La Brea Tar Pits, California, USA"),0xFFC9A87C),
        DinoEntry("columbian-mammoth","Columbian Mammoth",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~1 million–11,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.MAMMOTH,"13–14 ft (4–4.3 m)","10 tons","Plains of North America","Larger and less hairy than the woolly mammoth, the Columbian mammoth roamed the warmer regions of North America. It stood 14 feet tall and had tusks up to 16 feet long. It coexisted with early humans who hunted it.",listOf("Discovered 1857","Larger than the woolly mammoth — 14 ft tall","Tusks up to 16 feet long","Roamed warmer North American plains","Hunted by early humans"),listOf("Rancho La Brea, California, USA","Texas, USA","Mexico"),0xFFB8906C),
        DinoEntry("pygmy-mammoth","Pygmy Mammoth",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~100,000–11,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.MAMMOTH,"5.5–7 ft (1.7–2.1 m)","1,600–2,000 lb","Channel Islands, California","A dwarf mammoth that evolved on the Channel Islands of California through insular dwarfism. The pygmy mammoth was less than half the height of its mainland ancestor — a dramatic example of island evolution shrinking large animals.",listOf("Discovered 1856","Dwarf mammoth — only 5.5 ft tall at the shoulder","Evolved through insular dwarfism on islands","Less than half the size of its ancestor","Found on California's Channel Islands"),listOf("Santa Rosa Island, California, USA"),0xFFB8A06C),
        DinoEntry("smilodon","Smilodon",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~2.5 million–10,000 years",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.SABERTOOTH,"6.5 ft (2 m)","600–900 lb","Plains and forests of the Americas","The iconic saber-toothed cat with 7-inch canine teeth used for a precise killing bite to the throat. Thousands of Smilodon skeletons from La Brea Tar Pits make it one of the best-known Ice Age predators. It was NOT closely related to modern tigers.",listOf("Discovered 1842","7-inch saber teeth for a precise throat bite","Over 2,000 skeletons from La Brea Tar Pits","California's state fossil","NOT closely related to modern tigers"),listOf("La Brea Tar Pits, Los Angeles, USA","Florida, USA","South America"),0xFFC97050),
        DinoEntry("smilodon-populator","Smilodon populator",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~1 million–10,000 years",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.SABERTOOTH,"8.2 ft (2.5 m)","700–1,000 lb","Plains of South America","The largest saber-toothed cat — larger and more powerfully built than the North American Smilodon fatalis. Smilodon populator was the largest cat ever and the apex predator of Ice Age South America, hunting giant ground sloths and toxodonts.",listOf("Discovered 1842","Largest saber-toothed cat — up to 1,000 lbs","Largest cat to ever exist","Apex predator of Ice Age South America","Hunted giant ground sloths"),listOf("Argentina","Brazil","Uruguay"),0xFFC96040),
        DinoEntry("dire-wolf","Dire Wolf",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~250,000–9,500 years",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.WOLF_PREHISTORIC,"5 ft (1.5 m)","130–150 lb","Plains of North America","Larger and more heavily built than a modern gray wolf, the dire wolf had the strongest bite of any canid. Over 4,000 dire wolf skulls have been recovered from La Brea Tar Pits. It went extinct about 9,500 years ago, outlasted by the smaller gray wolf.",listOf("Discovered 1858","Larger than a modern gray wolf with a stronger bite","Over 4,000 skulls from La Brea Tar Pits","Hunted in packs like modern wolves","Went extinct ~9,500 years ago"),listOf("La Brea Tar Pits, California, USA","Florida, USA","Indiana, USA"),0xFF8B7A60),
        DinoEntry("short-faced-bear","Giant Short-Faced Bear",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~800,000–11,000 years",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.BEAR_GIANT,"12 ft standing (3.7 m)","1,800–2,000 lb","Plains of North America","The largest bear ever — standing 12 feet tall on its hind legs. The giant short-faced bear was a fast runner that could reach 40 mph, making it the most terrifying predator of Ice Age North America. It may have stolen kills from other predators including dire wolves and Smilodon.",listOf("Discovered 1858","Largest bear ever — 12 ft standing on hind legs","Could run 40 mph — faster than a grizzly","Stole kills from other Ice Age predators","Most terrifying predator of Ice Age North America"),listOf("California, USA","Indiana, USA","Alaska, USA"),0xFF7A6A4A),
        DinoEntry("woolly-rhino","Woolly Rhinoceros",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~350,000–10,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.RHINO_GIANT,"11 ft (3.5 m)","3,000–5,000 lb","Tundra of Eurasia","A heavily-built Ice Age rhinoceros with thick fur, a fatty hump, and a massive flattened front horn used to sweep away snow while grazing. Cave paintings by Ice Age humans show it with a dark band around its midsection.",listOf("Discovered 1769","Massive front horn used to sweep away snow","Cave paintings show a dark band around its body","Thick fur and a fatty hump for cold","Coexisted with early humans who hunted it"),listOf("Siberia, Russia","Ukraine","England","France"),0xFFA08060),
        DinoEntry("elasmotherium","Elasmotherium",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~2.5 million–29,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.RHINO_GIANT,"20 ft (6 m)","4.5 tons","Steppes of Eurasia","The 'Siberian unicorn' — a giant rhinoceros with a single 6-foot horn on its forehead. Elasmotherium was the largest rhinoceros ever, with legs like a horse for running across steppes. It may have inspired the unicorn legend and survived until at least 29,000 years ago.",listOf("Discovered 1808","'Siberian unicorn' — 6-foot horn on its forehead","Largest rhinoceros ever — 20 ft long","May have inspired the unicorn legend","Survived until ~29,000 years ago"),listOf("Russia","Kazakhstan","China"),0xFF9A7A60),
        DinoEntry("cave-lion","Cave Lion",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~370,000–14,000 years",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.WOLF_PREHISTORIC,"11.5 ft (3.5 m)","880–1,100 lb","Tundra of Eurasia","Larger than a modern African lion, the cave lion was one of the largest cats ever. Cave paintings show it had no mane. Frozen cave lion cubs found in Siberian permafrost are so perfectly preserved that their fur, whiskers, and even paw pads are visible.",listOf("Discovered 1810","Larger than a modern African lion","Frozen cubs preserve fur, whiskers, and paw pads","Cave paintings show it had no mane","Hunted mammoths, bison, and cave bears"),listOf("Siberia, Russia","France","Germany"),0xFFB8905A),
        DinoEntry("cave-bear","Cave Bear",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~575,000–27,500 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.BEAR_GIANT,"11.5 ft standing (3.5 m)","1,000–2,000 lb","Caves of Europe","Larger than the largest modern brown bear, the cave bear spent winters hibernating in European caves. Tens of thousands of cave bear skeletons have been found in caves. Early humans competed with cave bears for shelter and may have contributed to their extinction.",listOf("Discovered 1774","Larger than the biggest modern brown bear","Tens of thousands of skeletons found in caves","Competed with early humans for cave shelter","Hibernated through Ice Age winters"),listOf("Austria","Germany","Romania","Russia"),0xFF7A6A5A),
        DinoEntry("american-lion","American Lion",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~340,000–11,000 years",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.WOLF_PREHISTORIC,"8 ft (2.5 m)","900 lb","Plains of North America","One of the largest cats ever — 25% larger than a modern African lion. The American lion ranged from Alaska to Peru. La Brea Tar Pits preserve many specimens. It may have been the largest cat to ever exist, though some give that title to Smilodon.",listOf("Discovered 1853","25% larger than a modern African lion","Ranged from Alaska to Peru","One of the largest cats ever","Hunted bison, horses, and possibly young mammoths"),listOf("La Brea Tar Pits, California, USA","Alaska, USA","Peru"),0xFFC9A05A),
        DinoEntry("american-cheetah","American Cheetah",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~2.6 million–11,000 years",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.WOLF_PREHISTORIC,"5 ft (1.5 m)","150 lb","Plains of North America","A long-legged cat that evolved cheetah-like body proportions independently. The American cheetah may explain why pronghorns can run 60 mph — they evolved to outrun this extinct predator. It went extinct at the end of the Ice Age.",listOf("Discovered 1894","Evolved cheetah-like speed independently","Pronghorns run 60 mph to escape this extinct predator","Convergent evolution with modern cheetahs","Hunted pronghorns on American plains"),listOf("Wyoming, USA","Nevada, USA","Texas, USA"),0xFFB8A05A),
        DinoEntry("homotherium","Homotherium (Scimitar Cat)",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~3 million–10,000 years",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.WOLF_PREHISTORIC,"6.5 ft (2 m)","400–500 lb","Plains of North America & Eurasia","A saber-toothed cat with shorter, serrated scimitar-like canine teeth. Homotherium was a fast runner that hunted in packs, unlike the ambush-hunting Smilodon. It had longer legs than modern cats and may have chased down young mammoths.",listOf("Discovered 1894","Scimitar-shaped serrated canine teeth","Fast runner that hunted in packs","Longer legs than modern cats","May have hunted young mammoths"),listOf("Texas, USA","Alaska, USA","England","Netherlands"),0xFF9A7A5A),
        DinoEntry("giant-beaver","Giant Beaver",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~1.4 million–11,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.BEAR_GIANT,"8 ft (2.4 m)","450–575 lb","Wetlands of North America","The largest beaver ever — the size of a black bear, with 6-inch incisors. Giant beaver built enormous lodges and dams. Unlike modern beavers, it may not have felled trees — its teeth were better suited for aquatic plants.",listOf("Discovered 1846","Largest beaver ever — size of a black bear","6-inch incisor teeth","Built enormous lodges and dams","May have eaten aquatic plants rather than wood"),listOf("Florida, USA","New York, USA","Minnesota, USA"),0xFF8B7A4A),
        DinoEntry("glyptodont","Glyptodon",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~2.5 million–11,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ANKYLOSAUR,"11 ft (3.3 m)","2 tons","Plains of South America","A giant armadillo the size of a Volkswagen Beetle, covered in a domed shell of fused bony plates. Glyptodon had a spiked tail club like an ankylosaur. It could not roll into a ball like modern armadillos — its armor was too rigid.",listOf("Discovered 1839","Giant armadillo the size of a VW Beetle","Shell of fused bony plates — like a turtle","Spiked tail club like an ankylosaur","Hunted by early humans"),listOf("Argentina","Brazil","Uruguay"),0xFF8B8A5A),
        DinoEntry("doedicurus","Doedicurus",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~2 million–11,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ANKYLOSAUR,"13 ft (4 m)","2 tons","Plains of South America","A glyptodont with a massive spiked tail club that could shatter bone. Doedicurus had the most extreme tail weapon of any mammal — a spiked club on a flexible tail that it could swing like a mace against predators or rivals.",listOf("Discovered 1867","Massive spiked tail club — could shatter bone","Most extreme tail weapon of any mammal","Shell up to 2 inches thick","Swung its tail like a mace"),listOf("Argentina","Uruguay","Brazil"),0xFF7A7A4A),
        DinoEntry("diprotodon","Diprotodon",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~1.6 million–46,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.BEAR_GIANT,"10 ft (3 m)","2.5–3 tons","Forests and plains of Australia","The largest marsupial ever — a wombat relative the size of a rhinoceros. Diprotodon weighed up to 3 tons and was the largest mammal in Ice Age Australia. Aboriginal Australians coexisted with diprotodons and may have hunted them.",listOf("Discovered 1863","Largest marsupial ever — size of a rhinoceros","Weighed up to 3 tons","Coexisted with Aboriginal Australians","A giant wombat relative"),listOf("Queensland, Australia","New South Wales, Australia","South Australia"),0xFF9A8A6A),
        DinoEntry("thylacoleo","Thylacoleo (Marsupial Lion)",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~2 million–46,000 years",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.SABERTOOTH,"5 ft (1.5 m)","220–280 lb","Forests of Australia","The 'marsupial lion' — the most specialized mammalian carnivore ever, with the strongest bite for its size of any mammal. Thylacoleo had blade-like premolars and a thumb claw for disemboweling prey. Despite its name, it was not closely related to true lions.",listOf("Discovered 1859","Strongest bite for its size of any mammal","Blade-like teeth and a disemboweling thumb claw","Not related to true lions — a marsupial","Most specialized mammalian carnivore ever"),listOf("Queensland, Australia","Victoria, Australia"),0xFF8A6A5A),
        DinoEntry("megaloceros","Megaloceros (Irish Elk)",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~400,000–8,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ORNITHOPOD,"7 ft (2.1 m)","1,200–1,500 lb","Forests and plains of Eurasia","The largest deer ever, with antlers spanning up to 12 feet from tip to tip and weighing up to 90 pounds. Despite the name 'Irish elk,' it was a giant fallow deer that ranged across all of Eurasia. Bogs in Ireland preserve spectacular complete skeletons.",listOf("Discovered 1847","Largest deer ever — 12-ft antler span","Antlers weighed up to 90 lbs","Despite the name, it was a fallow deer","Shed and regrew its enormous antlers annually"),listOf("Bogs of Ireland","Germany","Russia","China"),0xFFB8906A),
        DinoEntry("mastodon","Mastodon",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~5 million–10,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.MAMMOTH,"9–10 ft (2.7–3 m)","8–10 tons","Forests of North America & Central America","Distantly related to mammoths, mastodons had different teeth — cone-shaped for crushing twigs, not grazing grass. They browsed in forests rather than open tundra. Mastodons were hunted by early humans and went extinct about 10,000 years ago.",listOf("Discovered 1739","Different teeth from mammoths — for crushing twigs","Browsed in forests, not open tundra","Hunted by early humans","Went extinct ~10,000 years ago"),listOf("New York, USA","Michigan, USA","Florida, USA","Mexico"),0xFFA8805A),
        DinoEntry("steppe-bison","Steppe Bison",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~575,000–8,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ORNITHOPOD,"8 ft (2.4 m)","2,000 lb","Tundra of Eurasia & North America","The giant bison of the Ice Age, with horns spanning 6 feet from tip to tip. Steppe bison are preserved in cave paintings at Lascaux and other sites. A nearly complete mummy named 'Blue Babe' was found frozen in Alaska with intact fur and skin.",listOf("Discovered 1827","Horns spanning 6 feet tip to tip","'Blue Babe' — frozen mummy with intact fur and skin","Depicted in Lascaux cave paintings","Larger than modern bison"),listOf("Alaska, USA","France","Russia","Canada"),0xFF9A7A4A),
        DinoEntry("bison-antiquus","Bison antiquus",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~250,000–10,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ORNITHOPOD,"7.5 ft (2.3 m)","3,300 lb","Plains of North America","A large extinct bison — the direct ancestor of the modern American bison. Bison antiquus was about 15-20% larger than modern bison with longer horns. It was hunted by early Native Americans and is depicted in cave paintings.",listOf("Discovered 1852","Direct ancestor of the modern American bison","15-20% larger than modern bison","Hunted by early Native Americans","Depicted in cave paintings"),listOf("California, USA","Texas, USA","Alaska, USA"),0xFF8A7A4A),
        DinoEntry("camelops","Camelops",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~3.5 million–11,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ORNITHOPOD,"7 ft (2.1 m)","1,800 lb","Plains of North America","The 'yesterday's camel' — a large North American camel that went extinct about 11,000 years ago. Camelops was larger than a modern dromedary and roamed from Alaska to Mexico. All camels evolved in North America before migrating to Asia and South America.",listOf("Discovered 1854","'Yesterday's camel' — large Ice Age North American camel","All camels evolved in North America","Larger than a modern dromedary","Went extinct ~11,000 years ago"),listOf("Texas, USA","California, USA","Nebraska, USA"),0xFFA8906A),
        DinoEntry("hagerman-horse","Hagerman Horse",DinoEra.NEOGENE,"Pliocene","~3.5 million years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ORNITHOPOD,"5 ft (1.5 m)","1,000 lb","Plains of North America","An early horse with a single functional toe — the first truly modern-looking horse. The Hagerman horse is Idaho's state fossil. Hundreds of complete skeletons have been found at Hagerman Fossil Beds, representing an entire herd.",listOf("Discovered 1928","First truly modern-looking horse","Idaho's state fossil","Hundreds of skeletons found together — a whole herd","Single-toed like modern horses"),listOf("Hagerman Fossil Beds, Idaho, USA"),0xFF9A8A6A),
        DinoEntry("giant-horse","Equus giganteus",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~2.5 million–11,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ORNITHOPOD,"6.5 ft (2 m)","1,500 lb","Plains of North America","One of the largest horses ever — larger than a modern draft horse. Equus giganteus roamed Ice Age North America alongside mammoths and bison. All horses in the Americas went extinct about 11,000 years ago, only returning with European explorers.",listOf("Discovered 1858","One of the largest horses ever","All American horses went extinct ~11,000 years ago","Larger than a modern draft horse","Horses only returned to the Americas with Europeans"),listOf("Texas, USA","Nevada, USA","Idaho, USA"),0xFFB8906A),
        DinoEntry("toxodon","Toxodon",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~11 million–11,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.RHINO_GIANT,"9 ft (2.7 m)","3 tons","Plains of South America","A large hippo-like mammal with a bizarre skull — its nostrils faced upward, suggesting it may have been semi-aquatic. Toxodon was one of the most common large mammals of Ice Age South America. Darwin himself collected its fossils.",listOf("Discovered 1837 by Charles Darwin","Charles Darwin collected its fossils","Hippo-like with upward-facing nostrils","One of the most common South American Ice Age mammals","Classified by Richard Owen from Darwin's specimens"),listOf("Argentina","Brazil","Uruguay","Bolivia"),0xFF8A7A5A),
        DinoEntry("macrauchenia","Macrauchenia",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~7 million–10,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.ORNITHOPOD,"10 ft (3 m)","2,000 lb","Plains of South America","A strange South American mammal with a camel-like body, elephant-like legs, and a trunk like a tapir. Macrauchenia was a litoptern — a group of unique South American hoofed mammals with no living relatives. It went extinct about 10,000 years ago.",listOf("Discovered 1838 by Charles Darwin","Camel body, elephant legs, and a tapir-like trunk","No living relatives — a uniquely South American lineage","Charles Darwin collected its fossils","Could change direction quickly while running"),listOf("Argentina","Bolivia","Peru"),0xFFA8986A),
        DinoEntry("eremotherium","Eremotherium",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Pleistocene","~5 million–10,000 years",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.SLOTH_GIANT,"20 ft (6 m)","5 tons","Forests of North & South America","A giant ground sloth that ranged from the southern US to Brazil. Eremotherium was nearly as large as Megatherium and could rear up on its hind legs to browse 20 feet high. It went extinct about 10,000 years ago, likely due to human hunting.",listOf("Discovered 1850","Giant sloth ranging from the US to Brazil","Could rear up to browse 20 ft high","Nearly as large as Megatherium","Went extinct ~10,000 years ago"),listOf("Florida, USA","Brazil","Mexico"),0xFF8B8A6A),

        // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        // RECENTLY EXTINCT (Holocene)
        // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

        DinoEntry("thylacine","Thylacine (Tasmanian Tiger)",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Holocene","~1936 AD (extinct)",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.WOLF_PREHISTORIC,"5 ft (1.5 m)","45–70 lb","Tasmania, Australia","The largest carnivorous marsupial of modern times. The thylacine looked like a dog with tiger-like stripes and a pouch. The last confirmed individual died in Hobart Zoo in 1936. Unconfirmed sightings continue to this day.",listOf("Discovered 1808","Largest carnivorous marsupial of modern times","Last one died in Hobart Zoo in 1936","Males had a pouch too — unique among marsupials","Some people still search for surviving thylacines"),listOf("Tasmania, Australia","Mainland Australia (fossil)","New Guinea (fossil)"),0xFF8B7A60),
        DinoEntry("dodo","Dodo",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Holocene","~1681 AD (extinct)",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.BIRD_PREHISTORIC,"3.3 ft (1 m)","23–39 lb","Mauritius","The flightless pigeon of Mauritius — the icon of human-caused extinction. The dodo went extinct just 83 years after its discovery by Europeans. Despite its fame, almost no complete skeletons existed until the 19th century. It was not clumsy — it was well-adapted before humans arrived.",listOf("Discovered 1598","Extinct by 1681 — just 83 years after discovery","Actually a giant flightless pigeon — not clumsy","Almost no complete skeletons survive","Icon of human-caused extinction"),listOf("Mare aux Songes, Mauritius"),0xFFA08060),
        DinoEntry("great-auk","Great Auk",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Holocene","~1844 AD (extinct)",DinoDiet.PISCIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.BIRD_PREHISTORIC,"33 in (85 cm)","11 lb","North Atlantic coasts","The 'original penguin' — a large flightless seabird of the North Atlantic. The great auk was hunted to extinction for its feathers, oil, and eggs. The last confirmed pair was killed on Eldey Island in 1844, and their single egg was crushed underfoot.",listOf("Discovered 1813","The 'original penguin' of the Northern Hemisphere","Hunted to extinction by 1844","Last pair killed and their egg crushed","Gave the name 'penguin' to southern birds"),listOf("Iceland","Newfoundland, Canada","Scotland"),0xFF7A7A8A),
        DinoEntry("moa","Giant Moa",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Holocene","~1445 AD (extinct)",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.BIRD_PREHISTORIC,"12 ft (3.7 m)","500 lb","New Zealand","The tallest bird ever, reaching 12 feet — with no wings at all, not even vestigial ones. Native to New Zealand, the giant moa was hunted to extinction by the M\u0101ori by about 1445 AD. Complete skeletons with soft tissue have been found in caves.",listOf("Discovered 1839","Tallest bird ever — 12 ft with no wings at all","Hunted to extinction by M\u0101ori by ~1445 AD","Complete skeletons with soft tissue found in caves","No wing bones whatsoever"),listOf("South Island, New Zealand","North Island, New Zealand"),0xFF8A7060),
        DinoEntry("stellers-sea-cow","Steller's Sea Cow",DinoEra.QUATERNARY,"Holocene","~1768 AD (extinct)",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.BEAR_GIANT,"30 ft (9 m)","10 tons","Commander Islands, Bering Sea","A giant sirenian related to dugongs that was discovered alive in 1741 and hunted to extinction by 1768 — just 27 years later. Steller's sea cow could not submerge and was easy prey for sailors. It is one of the most recently extinct large animals.",listOf("Discovered 1741","Discovered alive in 1741 — extinct by 1768","Could not submerge — floated at the surface","Hunted to extinction in just 27 years","One of the most recently extinct large animals"),listOf("Commander Islands, Bering Sea"),0xFF8B8A7A),

        // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        // BEFORE THE DINOSAURS
        // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

        DinoEntry("dimetrodon","Dimetrodon",DinoEra.OTHER,"Early Permian","~295–272 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.SYNAPSID,"15 ft (4.6 m)","550 lb","Swamps of North America & Europe","NOT a dinosaur — a synapsid (mammal ancestor) that lived 40 million years before the first dinosaur. Dimetrodon had a massive sail on its back and was the apex predator of the Permian. It is more closely related to you than to any dinosaur.",listOf("Discovered 1878","NOT a dinosaur — a mammal ancestor","More closely related to YOU than to any dinosaur","Had a sail on its back for display or temperature regulation","Apex predator 40 million years before dinosaurs","Often mislabeled as a dinosaur in toy sets"),listOf("Texas, USA","Oklahoma, USA","Germany"),0xFF5A7A6A),
        DinoEntry("edaphosaurus","Edaphosaurus",DinoEra.OTHER,"Late Carboniferous–Early Permian","~300–280 mya",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.SYNAPSID,"11 ft (3.5 m)","600 lb","Swamps of North America & Europe","A sail-backed synapsid like Dimetrodon, but a plant-eater. Edaphosaurus had a sail with cross-bars — different from Dimetrodon's smooth-spined sail. It had peg-like teeth for grinding plants and may have been the first large herbivore.",listOf("Discovered 1878","A sail-backed plant-eater — first large herbivore","Sail had cross-bars unlike Dimetrodon's smooth sail","Peg-like teeth for grinding plants","Mammal ancestor, not a dinosaur"),listOf("Texas, USA","New Mexico, USA","Czech Republic"),0xFF6A8A6A),
        DinoEntry("diplocaulus","Diplocaulus",DinoEra.OTHER,"Early Permian","~295–270 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.CROCODILIAN,"3 ft (1 m)","20 lb","Swamps of North America","An amphibian with one of the strangest skulls in paleontology — a wide boomerang shape with two long lateral horns. The bizarre skull may have acted as a hydrofoil for swimming, or made it hard for predators to swallow.",listOf("Discovered 1878","Boomerang-shaped skull — one of the strangest ever","Skull may have acted as a hydrofoil for swimming","Horns may have prevented predators from swallowing it","A popular fossil for collectors"),listOf("Texas Red Beds, USA","Oklahoma, USA"),0xFF80A060),
        DinoEntry("eryops","Eryops",DinoEra.OTHER,"Early Permian","~295 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.CROCODILIAN,"6 ft (2 m)","200 lb","Swamps of North America","A crocodile-sized amphibian with a massive broad skull and sharp teeth. Eryops was one of the largest land animals of the early Permian. It looked like a cross between a crocodile and a salamander, hunting fish and small tetrapods in swamps.",listOf("Discovered 1884","Crocodile-sized amphibian — 6 ft long","One of the largest land animals of the early Permian","Massive broad skull with sharp teeth","Hunted in Permian swamps"),listOf("Texas Red Beds, USA","New Mexico, USA"),0xFF90B070),
        DinoEntry("anomalocaris","Anomalocaris",DinoEra.OTHER,"Middle Cambrian","~508 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.CROCODILIAN,"3.3 ft (1 m)","10 lb","Seas of North America & Asia","The world's first super-predator. Anomalocaris was a 3-foot swimming arthropod with large compound eyes, grasping frontal appendages, and a circular mouth of serrated plates. It ruled the Cambrian seas during the explosion of complex life.",listOf("Discovered 1892","World's first super-predator","Compound eyes with 16,000 lenses","Mouthparts mistaken for a jellyfish for decades","Apex predator of the Cambrian explosion"),listOf("Burgess Shale, British Columbia, Canada","Chengjiang, China"),0xFF8B7355),
        DinoEntry("dunkleosteus","Dunkleosteus",DinoEra.OTHER,"Late Devonian","~382–358 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.SHARK_GIANT,"20–33 ft (6–10 m)","3–4 tons","Seas of North America & Europe","A massive armored fish with self-sharpening bony plates instead of teeth. Dunkleosteus was the apex predator of the Devonian 'Age of Fishes.' Its bite created suction that could suck prey in. It could open its mouth in 1/60 of a second.",listOf("Discovered 1873","Armored fish with self-sharpening bony plates instead of teeth","Could open its mouth in 1/60 of a second","Apex predator of the Devonian Age of Fishes","Bite created suction to suck in prey"),listOf("Ohio, USA","Pennsylvania, USA","Belgium","Morocco"),0xFF5A6A8A),
        DinoEntry("tiktaalik","Tiktaalik",DinoEra.OTHER,"Late Devonian","~375 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.CROCODILIAN,"9 ft (3 m)","200 lb","Rivers of North America","The iconic 'fish with wrists' — a transitional fossil between fish and land vertebrates. Tiktaalik had fish-like fins but with wrist bones that could support its body, allowing it to push up out of shallow water. It could breathe air.",listOf("Discovered 2004 by Neil Shubin","The 'fish with wrists' — a critical transitional fossil","Had wrist bones in its fins — the first step toward walking","Could breathe air and push up out of water","Targeted search in Arctic Canada found it"),listOf("Ellesmere Island, Nunavut, Canada"),0xFF5A8A7A),
        DinoEntry("acanthostega","Acanthostega",DinoEra.OTHER,"Late Devonian","~365 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.CROCODILIAN,"2 ft (0.6 m)","8 lb","Swamps of Greenland","One of the first vertebrates with limbs and digits — but with 8 fingers on each hand. Acanthostega was still primarily aquatic and could not walk on land effectively. Its limbs were for navigating swampy vegetation, not walking.",listOf("Discovered 1952","One of the first vertebrates with limbs and digits","Had 8 fingers on each hand — more than any modern animal","Still primarily aquatic — limbs for swamps","Could not walk effectively on land"),listOf("Greenland"),0xFF6A9A7A),
        DinoEntry("eurypterid","Sea Scorpion (Eurypterid)",DinoEra.OTHER,"Silurian","~443–419 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.CROCODILIAN,"8.5 ft (2.6 m)","200 lb","Seas of North America & Europe","Sea scorpions were the largest arthropods ever — Jaekelopterus reached 8.5 feet. These ocean predators had pincers and a spiked tail. They ruled the seas before fish evolved jaws. New York's state fossil is a eurypterid.",listOf("Discovered 1818","Largest arthropods ever — up to 8.5 ft","Ruled the seas before fish evolved jaws","New York's state fossil","Had pincers and a spiked tail"),listOf("New York, USA","Ontario, Canada","England"),0xFF5A7A8A),
        DinoEntry("meganeura","Meganeura",DinoEra.OTHER,"Carboniferous","~305 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.PTEROSAUR,"28 in wingspan (70 cm)","1 lb","Swamp forests of Europe","A giant griffinfly — ancestor of dragonflies — with a 28-inch wingspan. Meganeura flew through Carboniferous forests when oxygen levels were 35% (vs 21% today), allowing insects to grow to enormous sizes. The largest flying insect ever.",listOf("Discovered 1880","Largest flying insect ever — 28-inch wingspan","Giant size enabled by 35% oxygen levels","Ancestor of modern dragonflies","Wingspan like a modern crow"),listOf("Commentry, France","England"),0xFF70A0C9),
        DinoEntry("arthropleura","Arthropleura",DinoEra.OTHER,"Carboniferous","~315–299 mya",DinoDiet.HERBIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.CROCODILIAN,"8.5 ft (2.6 m)","200 lb","Swamp forests of North America & Europe","The largest land arthropod ever — an 8.5-foot millipede that crawled through Carboniferous coal swamps. Arthropleura trackways have been found in Scotland. It was likely herbivorous, eating decaying vegetation.",listOf("Discovered 1853","Largest land arthropod ever — 8.5 ft long","Actually a millipede — and likely herbivorous","Giant size enabled by high Carboniferous oxygen","Trackways show it moved quickly"),listOf("Scotland, UK","Eastern Canada","Pennsylvania, USA"),0xFF6080A0),
        DinoEntry("pulmonoscorpius","Pulmonoscorpius",DinoEra.OTHER,"Carboniferous","~335 mya",DinoDiet.CARNIVORE,DinoBodyPlan.CROCODILIAN,"28 in (70 cm)","11 lb","Swamp forests of Scotland","A giant scorpion reaching 28 inches — larger than a house cat. Pulmonoscorpius had book lungs allowing it to breathe air, an advantage in the high-oxygen Carboniferous. It hunted in the coal swamps of ancient Scotland.",listOf("Discovered 1984","Giant scorpion larger than a house cat","Had book lungs — could breathe air","Hunted in Carboniferous coal swamps","One of the largest scorpions ever"),listOf("East Kirkton, West Lothian, Scotland"),0xFF507090),
    )
}
